# The documentations of Slabs share one site, docs.spomky-labs.com, which holds one directory per
# project. This file sends every address of the two former domains to its place there, path kept:
#
#   web-token.spomky-labs.com/4.3/the-components/claim-checker/
#       -> docs.spomky-labs.com/jwt/4.3/the-components/claim-checker/
#
# Both domains point at this hosting, so each rule tests the host it answers for. No rule applies to any
# other host: a site sharing this document root is left alone.
#
# In a .htaccess the pattern sees the path without its leading slash, so "$1" is the whole of it. The
# query string travels on its own, mod_rewrite appending it when the substitution carries none. A request
# for "/" gives an empty "$1" and lands on the project, which redirects to its latest version.
#
# These rules come first in the file: whatever follows must not answer for these two hosts.

RewriteEngine On

# JWT Framework, formerly web-token.spomky-labs.com
RewriteCond %{HTTP_HOST} ^web-token\.spomky-labs\.com$ [NC]
RewriteRule ^(.*)$ https://docs.spomky-labs.com/jwt/$1 [R=301,L]

# WebAuthn Framework, formerly webauthn-doc.spomky-labs.com
RewriteCond %{HTTP_HOST} ^webauthn-doc\.spomky-labs\.com$ [NC]
RewriteRule ^(.*)$ https://docs.spomky-labs.com/webauthn/$1 [R=301,L]
